30,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Takumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The staffing situation is precarious. The late-turn pool has thinned significantly, and roadway and signals crews are well below establishment, though lift and escalator maintenance has spare capacity.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"Interestingly, the mid-city runs are slower than they used to be, but the crowds are clearing at the same rate. Also, the audits show that waiting for connections is the real time-sink, not the ticketing machines.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The capital ledger contains a ring-fenced construction balance from a lapsed grant, but the operating subsidy and technology lines have been trimmed. Meanwhile, ticketing contractor fees are now exceeding our resurfacing budget.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The past offers some hope. A late-evening timetable pilot from eight years ago met its cost-recovery target, and while some lane-priority projects failed, several lift and entrance rebuilds were completed successfully.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest caution. Bidders are pricing in heavy contingencies for work in pre-war shells due to unrecorded voids, and the ferry and rail offices have never agreed on common passenger data terms.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, if the technology line has been trimmed and contractor fees are so high, any plan requiring a massive new digital overhaul seems financially risky.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, that makes sense. If the time is lost at connections and not at the machines, maybe we don't need a fancy new system to fix the passenger experience.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely, Takumi. It would be a logical fallacy to invest in a digital solution for a problem that is fundamentally structural or operational, especially when our technology budget is shrinking.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are converging on a truth. If the digital path is fraught with cost and the passenger's pain is not there, we should look toward the physical and the proven.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I must add that the physical path is not without its own traps. The pre-war shells are unpredictable, which makes any extensive structural work a gamble on the budget.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, if the pre-war shells are a gamble, then we must be very careful with any plan involving station rebuilds, unless we have the specific maintenance capacity to handle it.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, you mentioned that lift and escalator maintenance actually has spare capacity. Maybe that makes station work safer than we think?","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"That is an interesting correlation, Takumi. If the specialized labor is available, it might mitigate some of the risks Jonathan identified, provided the funding comes from the correct pot.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Takumi"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Servas,,It is heartening to see you connecting these dots. We must balance the risks of the unknown with the strengths we actually possess.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I suppose that is true, though spare capacity in maintenance does not necessarily erase the risk of unrecorded voids in the foundations. We must still be cautious.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"We must also consider the crews. If roadway and signals staff are depleted, any plan relying on new bus lanes or signal changes will likely fail or cause further instability.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, that's a huge point. If we can't actually staff the new lanes or the signals, then a bus corridor is just a fancy drawing that won't actually move people faster.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are systematically eliminating the impractical. If the budget for technology is gone and the manpower for roadway signals is absent, our options are narrowing toward the feasible.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Servas,,Indeed. We are distilling our choices by weighing the evidence of our resources against the ghosts of our failures. It is a most rational way to proceed.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. We have a clearer picture now: we must avoid digital overhauls and risky roadway expansions, while remaining mindful of the structural uncertainties in our oldest stations.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Takumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"The candidates are now before us. Given the staffing shortages in roadway crews and the budget cuts to technology, I cannot support the Crosstown Bus Corridor or the Integrated Fare Platform.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"I agree with Asuka. The journey audits show the fare system isn't the bottleneck, and the roadway crew shortage makes the bus corridor a non-starter. I'm leaning toward the Station Access Upgrades.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The Integrated Fare Platform is a financial fantasy given the contractor fees and budget trims. Between the remaining two, the Station Access Upgrades align better with our available maintenance capacity.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Servas,,"I recall the success of previous lift and entrance rebuilds. Combined with the late-evening pilot's success, the Night Service Extension is tempting, but the Station Access Upgrades feel more sustainable.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The Night Service Extension would likely strain the already thinned late-turn pool. Given the risks in the pre-war shells, the Station Access Upgrades are a gamble, but they are the most viable option remaining.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, if the late-turn pool is already thinned, the Night Service Extension would be a disaster for the crews. Station Access Upgrades are the only logical choice.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"I'm with Asuka. If we can't staff the night shifts and we don't need a new fare system, the station upgrades are the only way to actually improve the passenger flow.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"I find myself in agreement. While the pre-war shells present a financial risk, the availability of maintenance capacity makes it a calculated gamble rather than a blind leap.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Servas,,"It is a rare moment of total consensus. When the evidence of the past and the reality of the present align so clearly, the path forward becomes evident.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I concur. While I remain wary of the structural unknowns, the other options are simply untenable given our staffing and budgetary constraints. I will cast my vote for the Station Access Upgrades.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,I will also vote for the Station Access Upgrades. It is the only plan that respects our current staffing limitations while utilizing the capacity we actually have.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Takumi,,My vote goes to the Station Access Upgrades. It's the only plan that actually addresses the physical movement of people without relying on crews we don't have.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"I shall cast my vote for the Station Access Upgrades. It is the most logically sound choice, balancing our available resources against the constraints of our budget.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Servas,,I will join the consensus and vote for the Station Access Upgrades. It is a prudent choice that honors both our history and our current capabilities.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
